\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Repository asset showing the apple motion-editing case used as the primary qualitative example.}}{18}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Recorded flow-loss trajectories for the apple ablation. RAFT-guided primitive diffusion reaches lower flow loss than the no-RAFT variant across the three seeds.}}{32}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Total guidance-energy trajectories for the apple ablation. RAFT-guided variants have lower final energy than the no-RAFT diffusion condition.}}{33}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Final saved apple outputs. Rows are seeds 0--2. Columns are warp-inpaint-only, original Motion Guidance, diffusion without RAFT, diffusion with RAFT, and full pipeline. The full pipeline is visually strong, but its final image is composited.}}{33}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Pre-composite apple outputs for primitive diffusion variants. Rows are seeds 0--2. Columns are no-RAFT diffusion, RAFT-guided diffusion, and full-pipeline pre-composite output. These images separate diffusion behavior from final compositing.}}{34}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Additional teapot translation case over three seeds. Columns show source, warped initialization, decoded diffusion output, and final composited output.}}{36}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Additional tree file-flow squeeze case over three seeds. This case is useful as a stress test; it should not be presented as a polished rigid-translation result.}}{37}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.7}{\ignorespaces Representative apple outputs from the compact hyperparameter study. The grid shows source, hard-warp initialization, decoded diffusion output, and final pre-composite output for each setting.}}{39}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.8}{\ignorespaces Apple source-hole restoration comparison using the same moved object and source-hole mask. LaMa is visually cleaner than the local, directional, and OpenCV fills in this case and is therefore used as the final selected restoration.}}{40}{}%
